Crystal algorithm for conical-intersection seam space
====================================================

Pandey, A.; Poirier, B.; Liang, R. Development of Parallel On-the-Fly Crystal Algorithm for Global
Exploration of Conical Intersection Seam Space. J. Chem. Theory Comput. 2024, 20 (11), 4778-4789.
doi:10.1021/acs.jctc.4c00292

This directory holds the stilbene and 1,3-butadiene seam searches.

Differences from the reaction-discovery algorithm
-------------------------------------------------

The driver and worker machinery are those of ../reaction_discovery_2025, with two changes:

  1. Two cutoffs. A lattice point is accepted only if the gap between the two intersecting states
     is below the seam cutoff seamCut, and the average of the two state energies is below the
     energy cutoff that is raised shell by shell. seamCut is present but unused in the
     reaction-discovery drivers.

  2. Refinement optimizes minimum-energy conical intersections rather than minima, using DL-FIND
     through TeraChem (run conical) with the Lagrange-Newton algorithm and a degeneracy threshold
     of 1e-4 Ha.

The rejected-point list also stores the gap and the average energy of each rejected point, so that
raising the cutoff promotes points without repeating the quantum-chemistry step; intersection
optimizations are started only from newly accepted lattice points; and fragmented structures are
removed by distance criteria.

Systems
-------

  stilbene/     d = 12, seamCut = 0.013, Ecut0 = -540.9, energy-cutoff increment 0.005.
                SA-2-CASSCF(2e,2o)/6-31G* through TeraChem. Contents: the driver p.f90 with its
                two immediate predecessors oldP.f90 and savep.f90; the worker template
                OldSecondary/ (s.f90, submit.sh, sbatch.sh, tc.in, ttc.in, ttc2.in); one generated
                worker secondaryi/; loadFoundlings.sh, saout.sh, submissions.sh, eSubmissions.sh;
                radiux.txt; and the run's own readme.

  butadiene/    d = 24, seamCut = 0.0165, Ecut0 = -154.706, increment 0.004, on a full Cartesian
                lattice at 1 A spacing. SA-3-CASSCF(4e,3o)/6-31G* through TeraChem. Contents: the
                driver p.f90, the worker directory secondary1/ (s1.f90 and s2.f90, submit.sh,
                ttc.in), the submission scripts, and example/, a self-contained copy of the run
                directory.

In these runs the worker constructs Cartesian coordinates analytically from the lattice
coordinates, so the reference geometry is contained in the worker source and no separate seed
geometry file is used.

Build
-----

Intel Fortran (ifort). gfortran 10 or newer also works with
-ffree-line-length-none -fallow-argument-mismatch.

  module load intel-oneapi

  cd stilbene
  ifort p.f90 -o a.out
  ( cd OldSecondary && ifort s.f90 -o a.out )

  cd ../butadiene
  ifort p.f90 -o a.out
  ( cd secondary1 && ifort s1.f90 -o a.out && ifort s2.f90 -o b.out )

Running
-------

Both systems call TeraChem: stilbene through tc.in and ttc.in, butadiene through ttc.in with
casscf yes, closed 13, active 3. The worker submit.sh runs the driver, then TeraChem, then the
accept-or-reject step. Set $TeraChem to a TeraChem installation before submitting.

Reference values from the paper
-------------------------------

  system         method                          d.o.f.   seam cutoff   accepted / rejected
  PST-1          FOMO-hh-TDA-DFT, B3LYP/6-31G*      8       0.015 au      2,818 / 29,165
  stilbene       SA-2-CASSCF(2e,2o)/6-31G*         12       0.013 au      5,200 / 63,390
  1,3-butadiene  SA-3-CASSCF(4e,3o)/6-31G*         24       0.0165 au     177,331 / 637,058
                                                                          16,991 / 628,701

Intersections are clustered hierarchically with average linkage on pairwise RMSD at a 0.5 A
cutoff, followed by an energy criterion of 0.015 au within each cluster, which separates
structures that are geometrically similar but energetically distinct. Structures are required to
be intact: every carbon pair connected through C-C bonds shorter than 3 A, and every hydrogen
bound to a carbon within 2.1 A. Intersection sets are compared on a plane of energy against the
largest eigenvalue of the Pietrucci-Andreoni contact matrix with n = 6 and m = 10.
